{"birds": [{"name": "Downy Woodpecker", "sci": "Dryobates pubescens", "n": 4, "first": "9:42 AM", "last": "9:59 AM", "ts": 1793976146, "mins": [582, 588, 591, 599], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "North America’s smallest woodpecker. It is small enough to forage on thin weed stalks and twigs that bigger woodpeckers can’t cling to.", "vox": "sounds/voice/dryobates-pubescens.mp3?v=1793112995", "snd": "sounds/dryobates-pubescens.mp3?v=1793113092", "sndby": "Thomas G. Graves, xeno-canto XC89855, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "downy", "cm": 16.0, "img": "cutouts/downy.png?v=1793045350", "w": 486, "h": 800, "len": 707.0, "v": 1793045350, "beak": [0.59, 0.1, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 53, "b": "AAAAAP/4AAD//AAA//wAAP/8IAD//DwA//wf4H/8D/x//Af+f/wH/3/8A/9//AP/f/wD/3/8B/9//A//f/wf/n/8P/x//H/8f/7//H/+//x////8f////D////x////8f////H////h////4f///+D////A////wP///4D///+A////gP///wD///8A///+AP///gD///wA///4AP//+AD///AA///wAP//sAD//4AA//8AAP//AAD//wAA//4AAP/+AAD//gAA//4AAP/8AAD//AAA="}}, {"name": "Fish Crow", "sci": "Corvus ossifragus", "n": 14, "first": "9:42 AM", "last": "10:07 AM", "ts": 1793976159, "mins": [582, 582, 591, 591, 592, 592, 592, 593, 593, 594, 594, 595, 606, 607], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "It looks just like an American Crow. The giveaway is the voice: a nasal, two-note “uh-uh” instead of a clear “caw.”", "vox": "sounds/voice/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793112991", "snd": "sounds/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793113105", "sndby": "Mike Nelson, xeno-canto XC130581, CC BY-NC-SA 3.0", "key": "fishcrow", "cm": 38.0, "img": "cutouts/fishcrow.png?v=1793051285", "w": 800, "h": 474, "len": 557.0, "v": 1793051285, "beak": [0.27, 0.08, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 19, "b": "AH4AAAD+AAAAPwAAAB+AAAAf4AAAH/AAAB/4AAAf/gAAD/4AAAf/AAAD/4AAAf/AAAD34P8Bs+D///Dg/////v////8AD///AAAP/w=="}}, {"name": "Northern Cardinal", "sci": "Cardinalis cardinalis", "n": 3, "first": "9:45 AM", "last": "9:46 AM", "ts": 1793976312, "mins": [585, 585, 586], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Female cardinals sing too, which is unusual for a North American songbird. During courtship the male feeds her seeds beak to beak.", "vox": "sounds/voice/cardinalis-cardinalis.mp3?v=1793112978", "snd": "sounds/cardinalis-cardinalis.mp3?v=1793113085", "sndby": "David Tattersley, xeno-canto XC1000248, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "cardinal", "cm": 22.0, "img": "cutouts/cardinal.png?v=1793031428", "w": 516, "h": 348, "len": 530.0, "v": 1793031428, "beak": [0.06, 0.28, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 22, "b": "AYAAAAeAAAAPgAAAH4AAAB/AAAA/4AAAf/gAAB/+AAAf/4AAH//AAA//8AAP//gAB//8AAf//AAD//+AAf//wEB/v/jwf3v+///+/z//vn945gA+MAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "House Finch", "sci": "Haemorhous mexicanus", "n": 45, "first": "9:46 AM", "last": "10:32 AM", "ts": 1793976405, "mins": [586, 586, 589, 590, 591, 596, 597, 598, 600, 601, 603, 603, 604, 604, 605, 605, 606, 607, 607, 607, 608, 609, 609, 610, 610, 611, 611, 612, 612, 616, 616, 617, 617, 620, 620, 620, 622, 624, 624, 625, 625, 626, 626, 632, 632], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Native to the West, house finches were released on Long Island in 1940 as pet-shop “Hollywood finches” and spread across the East. Redder males are favored by females.", "vox": "sounds/voice/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113005", "snd": "sounds/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113108", "sndby": "Ted Floyd, xeno-canto XC622745, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "housefinch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/housefinch.png?v=1793046043", "w": 800, "h": 581, "len": 662.0, "v": 1793046043, "beak": [0.12, 0.13, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "B+AAAAfwAQoP8AHOH/gB7wf8Af8D/gH/B/8A3wf/AP4H/4zgB//fwAP/zwAD//4AAf/+AAD//AAAf/gAAD/8AA/+/AAH4B4AB+APAB6AD4A4AAeA8AAAAMAAAAA="}}, {"name": "Dark-eyed Junco", "sci": "Junco hyemalis", "n": 5, "first": "9:47 AM", "last": "10:08 AM", "ts": 1793976458, "mins": [587, 592, 592, 595, 608], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Often called “snowbirds” because they show up as winter arrives. The white outer tail feathers flash as they flit away.", "vox": "sounds/voice/junco-hyemalis.mp3?v=1793113009", "snd": "sounds/junco-hyemalis.mp3?v=1793113154", "sndby": "Phoenix Birder, xeno-canto XC563632, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "junco", "cm": 15.5, "img": "cutouts/junco.png?v=1793035950", "w": 800, "h": 562, "len": 625.0, "v": 1793035950, "beak": [0.2, 0.22, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 22, "b": "AAAABgAAAAYA8AAMAfgAOAP8ADAD/gBxAf8AZwH/gMwB/8G4Af/j8AD/94AA//4AAH/8AAA//gAAH/4AAAf/AAQPh4AEPwHABfgA4A/QAHA+AAAA8AAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Eastern Bluebird", "sci": "Sialia sialis", "n": 1, "first": "9:50 AM", "last": "9:50 AM", "ts": 1793976609, "mins": [590], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Bluebirds nest in tree cavities, and their numbers crashed in the early 1900s when starlings and house sparrows took the holes. Volunteer nest-box trails helped them rebound.", "vox": "sounds/voice/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113046", "snd": "sounds/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113130", "sndby": "Robin Carter, xeno-canto XC1374, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "bluebird", "cm": 18.0, "img": "cutouts/bluebird.png?v=1793035226", "w": 800, "h": 609, "len": 744.0, "v": 1793035226, "beak": [0.69, 0.1, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 24, "b": "AAPwAAAH/AAAD/4AAA/4AAAf8AAAH/AAAD/wAAB/+AAAf/gDAP/wDgH/8BwD//B4A//h4Af/54AH/94AD/98AB//4AA/P4AAPH0AAHPhAABvgAAA3gAAAPgAAABwAAAA"}}, {"name": "Bald Eagle", "sci": "Haliaeetus leucocephalus", "n": 1, "first": "9:50 AM", "last": "9:50 AM", "ts": 1793976646, "mins": [590], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Bald Eagles add to the same nest year after year, so some grow into the biggest nests of any North American bird: hundreds of pounds of sticks.", "vox": "sounds/voice/haliaeetus-leucocephalus.mp3?v=1793113008", "snd": "sounds/haliaeetus-leucocephalus.mp3?v=1793113183", "sndby": "Ryan P. O&#039;Donnell, xeno-canto XC81952, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "baldeagle", "cm": 63.0, "img": "cutouts/baldeagle.png?v=1793977259", "w": 383, "h": 577, "len": 490.0, "v": 1793977259, "beak": [0.012, 0.085, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 48, "b": "D8AAAB/gAAB/8AAA//AAAP/4AAD//AAAh/8AAAP/wAAD//gAA//+AAP//4AH///AD///4A///+AP///wD///8A////gP///4B///+Af///wD///8A////AH///wA///8AD///gAf//4AD//8AA+//AAPP/4AB3/+AAf//wAH//8AB///AA/6fwAP+AAAD/gAAA/8AAAH/AAAD/wAAAf8AAAH+AAAB/gAAAf4AAAH/AAAB/wAAAP8AAAAAAAAAAAA"}}, {"name": "Blue Jay", "sci": "Cyanocitta cristata", "n": 2, "first": "9:52 AM", "last": "9:54 AM", "ts": 1793976752, "mins": [592, 594], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "That blue isn’t a pigment. Tiny structures in the feathers scatter light, which is why a crushed blue feather looks brown. Jays also bury acorns and help spread oak trees.", "vox": "sounds/voice/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793112993", "snd": "sounds/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793113328", "sndby": "Christopher McPherson, xeno-canto XC691163, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "bluejay", "cm": 28.0, "img": "cutouts/bluejay.png?v=1793031427", "w": 385, "h": 369, "len": 474.0, "v": 1793031427, "beak": [0.99, 0.17, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 31, "b": "AAAPgAAAD+AAAA/4AAAH+AAAD/8AAB//AAA//gAAf/AAAP/wAAH/8AAD//AAB//wAB//4AAf/+AAf//gAH//zAD//5wA//94Af+OcAP/8eAD9/fgA/l/gAPgHwAHgD4AHwA4AB8AeAA+AGAAfADgAHgAwADwAAAA4AAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Red-shouldered Hawk", "sci": "Buteo lineatus", "n": 1, "first": "9:53 AM", "last": "9:53 AM", "ts": 1793976823, "mins": [593], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its loud, repeated kee-ah call is a favorite for Blue Jays to copy, so a hawk you hear in the yard may really be a jay.", "vox": "sounds/voice/buteo-lineatus.mp3?v=1793193504", "snd": "sounds/buteo-lineatus.mp3?v=1793193566", "sndby": "Denis Provencher, xeno-canto XC808846, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "redshouldered", "cm": 43.0, "img": "cutouts/redshouldered.png?v=1793193489", "w": 523, "h": 800, "len": 863.0, "v": 1793193489, "beak": [0.987, 0.093, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 49, "b": "AAAH+AAAD/4AAA/+AAAf/wAAP/8AAD//AAA/+AAAf/AAAP/4AAH/+AAB//gAA//4AAf/+AAP//gAH//8AD///AB///wAf//4AP//+AD///gB///wAf//8AP//+AD///gA///wAf//8AH//+AD///AA///gAP//4AH//8AB///AAf//wAH//4AB//+AA///gAP//4AH//8AD/9/gA/+f4AP/AewD/g//gPwf/4D4H/4B+B3+AfgAcAHwADAD8AAAAeAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Carolina Wren", "sci": "Thryothorus ludovicianus", "n": 17, "first": "9:59 AM", "last": "10:20 AM", "ts": 1793977150, "mins": [599, 600, 601, 601, 602, 602, 602, 603, 603, 610, 610, 615, 616, 619, 619, 620, 620], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its song sounds like “teakettle, teakettle, teakettle,” and pairs often sing duets. It will nest in odd places: flowerpots, mailboxes, even old boots.", "vox": "sounds/voice/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113060", "snd": "sounds/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113075", "sndby": "Jacob Saucier, xeno-canto XC498646, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "wren", "cm": 13.0, "img": "cutouts/wren.png?v=1793046044", "w": 800, "h": 633, "len": 781.0, "v": 1793046044, "beak": [0.85, 0.21, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 25, "b": "OAAAADgAAAA8AAAAHgB+AB4B/+APA//wB5//AAf//gAH//4AB//+AAf//AAH//wAD//4AAP/+AAB//H/AP/f/wA///8AGf/cAB/7AA//8AAP/4AAP/8AAP9gAAD8AAAAcAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "White-throated Sparrow", "sci": "Zonotrichia albicollis", "n": 46, "first": "10:01 AM", "last": "10:32 AM", "ts": 1793977271, "mins": [601, 605, 612, 613, 613, 615, 615, 615, 616, 616, 617, 618, 619, 620, 620, 621, 621, 622, 622, 622, 623, 623, 623, 624, 624, 624, 625, 625, 625, 626, 626, 627, 627, 628, 628, 628, 629, 629, 630, 630, 630, 631, 631, 632, 632, 632], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "White-throats come in two color forms, white-striped and tan-striped, and almost always pair with the opposite form. Listen for “Oh-sweet-Canada, Canada, Canada.”", "vox": "sounds/voice/zonotrichia-albicollis.mp3?v=1793113071", "snd": "sounds/zonotrichia-albicollis.mp3?v=1793113140", "sndby": "Matt Wistrand, xeno-canto XC339648, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "whitethroated", "cm": 17.0, "img": "cutouts/whitethroated.png?v=1793035227", "w": 800, "h": 460, "len": 599.0, "v": 1793035227, "beak": [0.73, 0.14, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 18, "b": "AAB+AAAB/gAAB/8DAB/+BwB//h4B//54f//84P///8AAP/8AAA/8AAAD8AAAA/AAAA8AAAA/AAAAeAAAAeAAAAeAAAAHAAAA"}}, {"name": "Red-bellied Woodpecker", "sci": "Melanerpes carolinus", "n": 1, "first": "10:02 AM", "last": "10:02 AM", "ts": 1793977359, "mins": [602], "anim": null, "trunk": true, "fact": "The “red belly” is only a faint pink blush you will rarely see. The red cap is the giveaway. Its barbed tongue can reach about two inches past the bill tip.", "vox": "sounds/voice/melanerpes-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113012", "snd": "sounds/melanerpes-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113332", "sndby": "Bruce Lagerquist, xeno-canto XC713255, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "redbellied", "cm": 24.0, "img": "cutouts/redbellied.png?v=1793031431", "w": 400, "h": 800, "len": 516.0, "v": 1793031431, "beak": [0.52, 0.23, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 64, "b": ""}}, {"name": "American Goldfinch", "sci": "Spinus tristis", "n": 1, "first": "10:33 AM", "last": "10:33 AM", "ts": 1793979184, "mins": [633], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "One of the strictest vegetarians among birds. Goldfinches nest late in summer, when thistle seeds are ripe, and swap bright yellow for olive-brown in winter.", "vox": "sounds/voice/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113053", "snd": "sounds/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113090", "sndby": "Will Sweet, xeno-canto XC453934, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "goldfinch", "cm": 12.0, "img": "cutouts/goldfinch.png?v=1793031429", "w": 517, "h": 369, "len": 415.0, "v": 1793031429, "beak": [0.18, 0.15, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "APgAAAH4AAAD/AAAB/wAAAP+AAEB/wAHAf+ADgH/wBwB/8DwAf/h4AH/94AA//8AAH/8ABA/+AAAH/wAAB/+AAA/fhAAeA8AA/gHgAfgB8APAANAPAAAADgAAAA="}}], "also": [], "date": "Friday, November 6", "where": "", "updated": "10:33 AM", "sun": null, "code": "5bc784c730", "listen": {"state": "on", "since": null, "control": false}}